=== Etymology===

The scientific name ''Pulmonaria'' is derived from Latin {{Lang|la|pulmo}} (lung).<ref name=AZPLA>{{cite book|last=Coombes|first=Allen J.|title=The A to Z of plant names|year=2012|publisher=Timber Press|location=USA|isbn=978-1-60469-196-2|pages=[https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781604691962/page/312 312]|url-access=registration|url=https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781604691962/page/312}}</ref> In the times of [[sympathetic magic]], the spotted oval leaves of ''P.&nbsp;officinalis'' were thought to symbolize diseased, ulcerated lungs, and so were used to treat pulmonary infections. The common name in many languages also refers to lungs, as in English "lungwort", German {{Lang|de|Lungenkraut}}, French {{Lang|fr|herbe aux poumons}}, and Serbian {{Lang|sr|plućnjak}}. In some East European languages, the common name is derived from a word for honey, e.g. Russian {{Lang|ru-Latn|medunitza}}, Polish {{Lang|pl|miodunka}}, Serbian {{Lang|sr|meduniče}}, Bulgarian {{Lang|bg|медуница}}.

== Distribution and habitat ==

The species of the genus are distributed across [[Eurasia]].{{sfn|Meeus et al|2016}}

== Ecology ==

''Pulmonaria'' is an early spring flowering [[deciduous]] perennial, retaining its leaves till late winter, just before the new growth emerges.{{sfn|Avent|2012}}

=== Pests and diseases ===

''Pulmonaria'' species are used as food plants by the [[larva]]e of some [[Lepidoptera]] species. These include the [[case-bearer]] ''[[Coleophora pulmonariella]]'', which feeds exclusively on ''P.&nbsp;saccharata'', and the [[moth]] ''[[Ethmia pusiella]]'', which has been recorded on ''P.&nbsp;officinalis''.

==Cultivation==
[[File:The Soviet Union 1988 CPA 5967 stamp (Deciduous forest flowers. Unspotted lungwort).jpg|thumb|right|upright|''Pulmonaria'' on stamp of USSR]]
Of the known species of ''Pulmonaria'', only about eight are known in cultivation.{{sfn|Avent|2012}} Pulmonaria are used as ornamental garden plants, particularly ''[[Pulmonaria saccharata|P.&nbsp;saccharata]]'', ''[[Pulmonaria angustifolia|P.&nbsp;angustifolia]]''<ref name=RHSAZ /> and ''[[Pulmonaria longifolia|P.&nbsp;longifolia]]''. Others include ''P.&nbsp;affinis''.{{sfn|Avent|2012}}They are especially valued as [[groundcover]] in damp shaded areas, producing their blue and/or pink flowers in late winter and early spring, accompanied by dense clusters of heart-shaped leaves that are often strikingly mottled and marbled, throughout summer.

